{"name":"Blue Blouse","hex":"#94a4b9","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"},{"term":"blouse","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A shirt, typically loose and reaching from the neck to the waist.","A shirt for women or girls, particularly a shirt with buttons and often a collar; a dress shirt tailored for women."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To hang a garment in loose folds.","To tuck one's pants/trousers (into one's boots)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blouse"}],"entries":[{"title":"Blue Blouse","blocks":[{"text":"The Blue Blouse was an influential agitprop theatre collective in the early Soviet Union. Boris Yuzhanin created the first Blue Blouse troupe under the auspices of the Moscow Institute of Journalism in 1923. Their example encouraged similar workers' theatre companies across the country and worldwide; by 1927 there were more than 5,000 Blue Blouse troupes in the Soviet Union with more than 100,000 members. In the autumn of that year, the original troupe began a tour in Erwin Piscator's theatre in Berlin that provoked a rapid growth of agitprop troupes across Weimar Germany."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/%D0%90%D1%84%D0%B8%D1%88%D0%B0_%D0%B4%D0%B8%D1%81%D0%BF%D1%83%D1%82%D0%B0-%D0%BF%D0%BE%D0%BA%D0%B0%D0%B7%D0%B0_%D1%82%D0%B5%D0%B0%D1%82%D1%80%D0%B0_%D0%A1%D0%98%D0%9D%D0%AF%D0%AF_%D0%91%D0%9B%D0%A3%D0%97%D0%90.%D0%92_%D0%BF%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B5%D1%87%D0%BD%D0%B5_%D0%B0%D1%80%D1%82%D0%B8%D1%81%D1%82%D0%BE%D0%B2_-_%D0%9A%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%82%D0%BD%D0%B8%D1%86%D0%BA%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%9A.%D0%9C..jpg/330px-%D0%90%D1%84%D0%B8%D1%88%D0%B0_%D0%B4%D0%B8%D1%81%D0%BF%D1%83%D1%82%D0%B0-%D0%BF%D0%BE%D0%BA%D0%B0%D0%B7%D0%B0_%D1%82%D0%B5%D0%B0%D1%82%D1%80%D0%B0_%D0%A1%D0%98%D0%9D%D0%AF%D0%AF_%D0%91%D0%9B%D0%A3%D0%97%D0%90.%D0%92_%D0%BF%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B5%D1%87%D0%BD%D0%B5_%D0%B0%D1%80%D1%82%D0%B8%D1%81%D1%82%D0%BE%D0%B2_-_%D0%9A%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%82%D0%BD%D0%B8%D1%86%D0%BA%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%9A.%D0%9C..jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue_Blouse","wikidata":"Q4928898"},{"title":"Blouse","blocks":[{"text":"A blouse is a loose-fitting upper garment, mainly worn by workmen, peasants, artists, women, and children. It is typically gathered at the waist or hips so that it hangs loosely (\"blouses\") over the wearer's body. Today, the word most commonly refers to a girl's or woman's dress shirt, although there is considerable confusion between a true blouse and a women's shirt. It can also refer to a man's shirt if it is a loose-fitting style, though it rarely is. Traditionally, the term has been used to refer to a shirt which blouses out or (alternatively) has an unmistakably feminine appearance; in contemporary fashion, standard dress shirts of both genders commonly come in such a loose fit without being considered \"blouses\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Striped_Bow_Neck_Blouse_and_a_Navy_Blue_Mini_Skirt_%2818966025833%29.jpg/330px-Striped_Bow_Neck_Blouse_and_a_Navy_Blue_Mini_Skirt_%2818966025833%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blouse","wikidata":"Q152563"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/%D0%AD%D1%81%D1%82%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B4%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%B3%D1%80%D1%83%D0%BF%D0%BF%D0%B0_%D1%82%D0%B5%D0%B0%D1%82%D1%80%D0%B0_%D0%A1%D0%98%D0%9D%D0%AF%D0%AF_%D0%91%D0%9B%D0%A3%D0%97%D0%90._%D0%92%D1%82%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D1%81%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%B2%D0%B0_-_%D1%80%D1%83%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%B8%D1%82%D0%B5%D0%BB%D1%8C_%D0%B3%D1%80%D1%83%D0%BF%D0%BF%D1%8B_%D0%9A%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%82%D0%BD%D0%B8%D1%86%D0%BA%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%9A.%D0%9C._1928_%D0%B3.jpg/500px-thumbnail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/%D0%AD%D1%81%D1%82%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B4%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%B3%D1%80%D1%83%D0%BF%D0%BF%D0%B0_%D1%82%D0%B5%D0%B0%D1%82%D1%80%D0%B0_%D0%A1%D0%98%D0%9D%D0%AF%D0%AF_%D0%91%D0%9B%D0%A3%D0%97%D0%90._%D0%92%D1%82%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D1%81%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%B2%D0%B0_-_%D1%80%D1%83%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%B8%D1%82%D0%B5%D0%BB%D1%8C_%D0%B3%D1%80%D1%83%D0%BF%D0%BF%D1%8B_%D0%9A%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%82%D0%BD%D0%B8%D1%86%D0%BA%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%9A.%D0%9C._1928_%D0%B3.jpg/500px-thumbnail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Blue Blouse troupe, 1928","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A%D0%AD%D1%81%D1%82%D1%80